<h1>Conveyor Systems by Industry: Food, Packaging, Pharma &amp; More</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Every industry imposes its own demands on conveyor systems: sanitation standards, load capacities, speed requirements, material compatibility, and regulatory compliance. These guides break down conveyor selection criteria by industry so you can match equipment to your specific application.</p>
<div class="photo-row"><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/food-flexlink-bread-loaf-conveyors.webp" width="960" height="600" alt="Bread loaves travel in single file on two white plastic chain conveyors with stainless steel frames and rod guide rails in a bakery." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Bread loaves on plastic chain conveyors with stainless steel frames and guide rails. Conveyors that carry unwrapped food are the ones that need food-contact belt materials and hygienic frame design. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.flexlink.com/en/home/industries/food" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FlexLink</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/warehouse-vanderlande-posisorter-shoe-sorter.webp" width="1000" height="666" alt="Cardboard cartons travel down a slatted sorter while a diagonal row of black shoes pushes one carton toward wheel-bed takeaway lanes at the side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Vanderlande POSISORTER sliding shoe sorter. Shoes slide across the slats and push each carton off to a takeaway lane on either side. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.vanderlande.com/systems/sortation/posisorter-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vanderlande</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/automotive-geely-overhead-carriers.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="Painted car bodies hang from yellow overhead carriers in a line while workers in blue uniforms work at the rear of the nearest body." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Car bodies hang from overhead carriers on a Geely assembly line in Ningbo, China. Overhead transport leaves the floor clear, so operators can reach the body from all sides. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geely_assembly_line_in_Beilun,_Ningbo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siyuwj, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
<h2 id="overview-heading">Why Industry Matters for Conveyor Selection</h2>
<p>A conveyor that works perfectly in a warehouse distribution center will fail in a food plant. Material construction, surface finish, cleaning protocols, environmental ratings, and regulatory certifications vary dramatically between industries. A stainless steel washdown conveyor with IP69K-rated motors and sensors costs significantly more than a painted carbon steel unit built for dry warehouse use, but in a food plant the equipment must be cleanable and corrosion-resistant under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/117.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA 21 CFR 117.40</a>.</p>
<p>These industry guides help you identify which <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types</a> match your application, and which specifications matter most. Each guide covers material selection, sanitation and compliance requirements, speed and throughput considerations, and cost factors specific to that industry.</p>
