<h2 id="criteria-heading">How to Choose a Conveyor by Industry</h2>
<p>Every conveyor specification starts with the same core questions, but the answers, and the weight you assign each factor, shift by industry. A food plant manager prioritizes sanitation and regulatory compliance above throughput. A warehouse operations manager prioritizes speed and sort accuracy above material construction.</p>
<p><strong>Start with these five factors:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product characteristics:</strong> Weight, dimensions, fragility, temperature, and surface type determine belt/roller material and conveyor width.</li>
<li><strong>Environment:</strong> Wet washdown, dry ambient, cleanroom, high-temperature, or outdoor exposure dictates frame material and IP rating.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory requirements:</strong> FDA food-contact rules, USDA or 3-A sanitary design, cGMP, MSHA (for example <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/30/56.14107" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 CFR 56.14107</a> guarding), or OSHA requirements constrain your material and design options. <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> is the general conveyor safety standard across industries.</li>
<li><strong>Throughput and speed:</strong> Products per minute, line speed in FPM, and accumulation requirements determine conveyor type and drive configuration.</li>
<li><strong>Integration:</strong> Upstream and downstream equipment connections, controls architecture, and facility layout define conveyor length, elevation, and transfer points.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> breaks down how each of these factors affects pricing. The <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a> covers controls and integration in detail.</p>
